# Suggested Chart Changes for istio-policy
# =========================================
#
# This folder contains proposed patches to the `edge-orch/common/charts/istio-policy`
# chart (currently v26.0.2) to address security gaps in east-west traffic control.
#
# Changes Summary:
#
# 1. templates/istio-policy.yaml
#    - PeerAuthentication mode is now configurable via values (`.Values.peerAuthentication.mode`)
#    - Default changed from PERMISSIVE → STRICT
#    - This enforces mTLS for ALL pod-to-pod communication within the mesh
#
# 2. templates/traffic-policy.yaml
#    - AuthorizationPolicies now include `from.source.namespaces` restrictions
#    - Only specific namespaces can reach each other (principle of least privilege)
#    - Example: orch-database only accepts traffic from orch-infra and orch-platform on port 5432
#
# 3. templates/network-policies.yaml (NEW)
#    - Kubernetes NetworkPolicies as defense-in-depth (CNI-level enforcement)
#    - Default-deny ingress per namespace + explicit allow rules
#    - Works even if Istio sidecars are absent (belt-and-suspenders approach)
#    - Controlled by `.Values.networkPolicy.enabled`
#
# 4. values-override.yaml
#    - Shows the values file changes needed for post-orch/values/istio-policy.yaml
#    - Adds `peerAuthentication.mode: STRICT`
#
# How to Apply:
#   Option A: Merge these templates into the upstream chart source
#   Option B: Apply as a separate Helmfile release alongside istio-policy
#
# Impact Assessment:
#   - PeerAuthentication STRICT: Pods without sidecars will be REJECTED (not just downgraded)
#     → Must ensure all pods have sidecars first (rolling restart)
#   - AuthorizationPolicy tightening: Services calling across unexpected namespaces will fail
#     → Test thoroughly in staging
#   - NetworkPolicies: Additive security; won't break if Istio is functioning correctly
#     → Safe to deploy first as validation layer
#
# Recommended Rollout Order:
#   1. Rolling restart all deployments (get sidecars injected)
#   2. Deploy NetworkPolicies (additive, non-breaking if traffic is already correct)
#   3. Deploy tightened AuthorizationPolicies (may break unexpected cross-namespace calls)
#   4. Switch PeerAuthentication to STRICT (breaks any non-mTLS communication)
